be to basically  create a narrative that there are  foreign forces trying to harm  turkey and with this narrative the  government tries to gather the remaining  supporters but this is not going to help  certainly not to to reach a turning  point for the ailing economy  so you say the government does not have  a road map what about the turkish  central bank it's just reduced its  policy rate uh the state banks are  following suit uh both a rather unusual  step in this current situation what's  the rationale behind this move  well first of all let me mention uh well  the turkish central bank announces 200  basis points drop in its policy rate but  there is a broad consensus that the  president himself of the turkish  republic gave an order to reduce the  interest rate so there is no independent  central bank in turkey anymore and the  logic of the government seems to be  there is a hope that with this low  interest rate the lira will weaken and  therefore turkish exports will in the  coming months increase be supported and  simultaneously lower interest rates in  turkey will make credits cheaper and  this should support domestic demand  enhanced growth however you know from  this is a very simplified assessment of  the economic relationship  the major problem of turkey is its high  inflation  and unfortunately the policy decision of  the central bank or the president's  decision is going to spur inflation  further on this is going to harm the  turkish economy more just to give you  some examples what will happen is people  are going to change their savings into  dollar dollarization is going to  increase capital foreign investors are  going to leave the country this is going  to harm investment and this is going to  to increase the problems of the turkish  economy  uh just briefly you mentioned already  capital flight obviously is a problem  where does this leave the economy which  is already a ailing  well the direct effect will be a  continuous weakening of the turkish lira  and the problem of a weakening lyrics of  course turkish goods become cheaper but  turkey is importing a lot of goods in  particular on the energy sector and also  in the agriculture sector meaning that  the inflation is going to increase and  this again  is going to to  result in a higher capital flight no  investment is going to take in turkey  anymore or investment is declining as a  result output is shrinking and this  basically means prices will increase  inflation is again increasing so it's a  vicious circle it's only accelerating  the problems therefore from an economic  point of view it's an irrational step  it's a political decision it's not an  economic decision  professor yeltsin as always thank you so  much for sharing your insights with us  i thank you very much  and of course financial markets are also  following the events in turkey  congratulations bye for us at the  frankfurt stock exchange conrad against  the the euro the lira even lost  6  that is a new record low  what's the word on the floor  well of course this is causing lots of  nervousness lots of disruption on the  financial markets  you know ratings agencies are of course  on high alert as are many investors who  have put money into turkey for them the  question is  who wants to hold a loan who wants to  hold an asset in a currency that's as  unpredictable as the turkish lira and  for the many many german companies who  are operative in turkey with thousands  of employees there  the question of course in the long run  is  what shall we do with our engagement in  turkey shall we keep it or shall we  rather get out of the country all right  difficult situation conrad bush in  frankfurt thank you
 

